  "transcript": "# One-off session on implications of the Defence Investment Plan for UK industry\n\n## Direction is welcome, but detail is missing\n> Industry supports the DIP’s shift to autonomy, AI and readiness, but needs a detailed 10-year, funded programme to invest.\n\nLincoln Jopp: You welcomed the SDR and DIP direction. Can you give concrete examples where the DIP has turned into real orders or behaviour changes?\nKevin Craven: We welcome the pivot to integrated systems, autonomy and AI, and rapid procurement has happened—£1.4bn for defence AI and some rapid tech buys—but the DIP focuses granularity on the first four years and leaves the 10-year picture vague.\nJulian David: Tech members expected more orders, especially for SMEs. Our surveys show declines, funding delays hit most firms, and many are reassessing investment or recruitment for defence work.\nKevin Craven: Equipment spend actually fell by £5bn to March 2026 versus the prior year—industry sees rhetoric, but orders are hard to pin down for long-term investing.\nStephen Phipson: There are pockets of change—energetics, cheap one-way effectors, drones—bringing non-traditional suppliers in. But these are small compared with the scale the DIP implies.\nLincoln Jopp: So, strategic intent yes, but not enough medium-term certainty for big industrial investment?\nKevin Craven: Exactly. Intent isn’t the same as an investable, funded plan that lets firms scale production lines and skills over a decade.\n\n\n## SMEs still struggle to access MOD opportunity\n> SMEs face visibility, contracting, payment and clearance barriers; the SME office exists but is under-resourced.\n\nArnab Dutt: The DIP is welcome but high-level. Most small firms don't use pipeline documents to find contracts—only 13% do. 62% say finding public opportunities is very difficult.\nLincoln Jopp: Has the MOD’s SME hub helped?\nArnab Dutt: Engagement has been very sparse. Promised regular contact hasn’t materialised; many SMEs that tried working with MOD say they won’t again because of systemic barriers.\nKevin Craven: Some primes have improved—Lockheed Martin, BAE, Babcock are cited for better supplier terms—but progress is patchy and depends on the primes' own certainty.\nJulian David: MOD uses a non-standard SME definition and SMEs are being hit by changes in security-clearance sponsorship; firms have lost hundreds of clearances and are reapplying.\nJesse Norman: What do you want from the Defence Office for Small Business Growth?\nKevin Craven: It should be far more than signposting: scale it to the staff numbers proposed, publish best practice (like Commercial X) and actively champion SMEs across MOD procurement.\nStephen Phipson: They are meant to reach full operational capability by year-end. A single front door is a good idea, but it must advocate and measure impact, not just show-and-tell.\n\n\n## Money matters: backloading, efficiency claims and the 3% target\n> Industry needs a funded multi-year path to 3–3.5% GDP now; backloading and efficiency-dependent funding undermine investment and jobs.\n\nChair: The DIP backloads investment after 2030 and relies on £15bn partly from efficiency savings. Can industry bring spending forward if needed?\nKevin Craven: You can’t switch on production overnight. With advance notice we can build capacity, but new production lines take 2–4 years and need investable certainty now.\nIan Roome: If the next spending review fails to give a funded path to 3% of GDP, will industry still invest?\nStephen Phipson: No. Firms need certainty before they commit to factories and major programmes. The DIP gives themes but not the funded programmes that persuade investors.\nKevin Craven: On jobs: ADS estimates an extra 50,000 jobs at 3% and another 85,000 by 2035 at 3.5%. Those are real economic benefits tied to the funding path.\nJulian David: Delays create a learned behaviour of not investing here. Capital goes where there is clarity—US and European markets look more attractive right now.\nChair: So you want a clear path to 3.5% now, not delayed into a future spending review?\nKevin Craven: Yes. A firm, multi-year, funded signal to industry and allies is essential to maintain capacity, exports and allied confidence.\n\n\n## Skills, costs and industrial competitiveness\n> The UK has engineering talent and manufacturing capacity, but skills gaps, high costs and slow infrastructure block conversion and retention.\n\nMichelle Scrogham: Defence inflation is running above general inflation. Are workforce, supply chain uncertainty and the wider investment environment the drivers?\nKevin Craven: Yes. Plus policy choices like steel tariffs and other business costs hurt competitiveness. Government can help with skills and the business environment.\nStephen Phipson: The big issue is vocational skills and FE capacity. SMEs struggle to find colleges to place apprentices because apprentice funding hasn’t been reformed since 2016.\nJulian David: Tech skills are sought after and infrastructure (data centres, connections) is slow and costly here—planning delays mean firms invest elsewhere.\nMichelle Scrogham: Could we repurpose automotive factories as Germany has?\nStephen Phipson: Yes—automotive companies want to help, and capacity exists, but they need clear programmes to decide where to invest.\nMichelle Scrogham: Have you seen attacks on defence execs affecting the sector?\nKevin Craven: Yes. There’s been an escalation from protesting premises to targeting individuals. That harms morale and could deter people and investors.\n\n\n## Finance, AI and international instruments\n> Private finance can help, but DFIS must make defence investable; MoD should use NATO procurement more and align AI/tech funds to defence needs.\n\nFred Thomas: What should the Defence Finance and Investment Strategy clarify to unlock private capital?\nKevin Craven: It must show investable markets, sensible risk-sharing, and SME finance routes for working capital, bonding and insurance; defence infrastructure deals are attractive to private finance.\nStephen Phipson: Series B / growth-stage capital is a UK weakness. Start-up funding exists, but patient domestic capital to scale factories is thin; many deals go to US investors.\nFred Thomas: How current is the DIP on AI and emerging tech? Is Government procurement keeping pace?\nJulian David: The DIP needn’t chase every model. But Defence must move to software-first procurement and secure compute/comms so kit can be upgraded. SovAI and other funds exist, but they aren’t obviously tied to defence needs.\nJesse Norman: What about the UK definition of a ‘UK company’—how should sovereignty be judged?\nJulian David: Define it by capability, control and choice—what we call 'confident sovereignty'—not merely where a company is registered. That protects supply without blanket protectionism.\nChair: Final quick point: why isn't the MOD using the NATO Support and Procurement Agency more?\nKevin Craven: We used to be the 5th largest user of NSPA; now we're 8th while NSPA budgets have tripled. The UK should use it more; it helps scale procurement and access allies’ buying power.\nStephen Phipson: We don't need another procurement agency; better use of existing NATO mechanisms and merging finance vehicles (DSRB/MDM) where sensible would avoid duplication.\nChair: Thank you all. In short: the DIP sets a welcome direction, but industry needs funded, granular, multi-year plans, clearer engagement with SMEs, faster procurement, stronger skills and better use of international instruments.\n",
